{"rationale":"All supplied grids are only 3x5 and contain few removable leaves, so each test uses an exhaustive oracle directly implementing the stated rule: for each removal count, try every subset of `o` leaves and perform reachability by same-row/same-column jumps among remaining leaves. The first subset that disconnects S and T gives the required minimum; if none does, the answer is -1. This is independent of flow construction and catches incorrect special handling for the supplied dimensions and target row. The repeated independent assertions are intentionally equivalent because every test must be safe on every supplied input; I would drop redundant copies first, retaining one exhaustive oracle.","tests":[{"name":"test_exhaustive_disconnect","source":"def test_exhaustive_disconnect(run, x):
    from itertools import combinations
    g = x.split()[2:]
    s = next((i,j) for i,r in enumerate(g) for j,c in enumerate(r) if c == 'S')
    t = next((i,j) for i,r in enumerate(g) for j,c in enumerate(r) if c == 'T')
    o = [(i,j) for i,r in enumerate(g) for j,c in enumerate(r) if c == 'o']
    ans = -1
    for k in range(len(o)+1):
        for rem in combinations(o, k):
            a = [s,t] + [v for v in o if v not in rem]
            seen, q = {s}, [s]
            while q:
                u = q.pop()
                for v in a:
                    if v not in seen and (u[0] == v[0] or u[1] == v[1]):
                        seen.add(v); q.append(v)
            if t not in seen:
                ans = k; break
        if ans != -1: break
    assert run(x).strip() == str(ans)
